WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.921
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Proficiency.

2
00:00:05.221 --> 00:00:08.491
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.791 --> 00:00:17.724
Unit twenty-three: Translation and Meaning. In this unit, you will explore the challenges and art of translation.

4
00:00:18.717 --> 00:00:20.066
untranslatable.

5
00:00:20.766 --> 00:00:25.668
untranslatable means impossible to express exactly in another language.

6
00:00:25.968 --> 00:00:29.419
Some emotional words seem almost untranslatable.

7
00:00:30.119 --> 00:00:33.456
Your turn. Say: untranslatable.

8
00:00:36.156 --> 00:00:37.517
untranslatable.

9
00:00:38.517 --> 00:00:39.773
equivalence.

10
00:00:40.473 --> 00:00:45.654
equivalence means the degree to which a translation has the same meaning as the original.

11
00:00:45.954 --> 00:00:48.963
Perfect equivalence between languages is rare.

12
00:00:49.663 --> 00:00:53.465
Your turn. Say: equivalence.

13
00:00:56.165 --> 00:00:57.224
equivalence.

14
00:00:58.217 --> 00:00:59.322
fidelity.

15
00:01:00.022 --> 00:01:03.926
fidelity means faithfulness to the original text.

16
00:01:04.226 --> 00:01:07.677
Translators balance fidelity and readability.

17
00:01:08.377 --> 00:01:11.250
Your turn. Say: fidelity.

18
00:01:13.950 --> 00:01:14.962
fidelity.

19
00:01:15.950 --> 00:01:17.172
domestication.

20
00:01:17.872 --> 00:01:22.750
domestication means adapting a translation to feel natural in the target culture.

21
00:01:23.050 --> 00:01:26.745
Domestication replaces foreign references with familiar ones.

22
00:01:27.445 --> 00:01:30.562
Your turn. Say: domestication.

23
00:01:33.262 --> 00:01:34.344
domestication.

24
00:01:35.350 --> 00:01:36.409
foreignisation.

25
00:01:37.109 --> 00:01:40.757
foreignisation means keeping the foreign feel of the original text.

26
00:01:41.057 --> 00:01:44.241
Foreignisation preserves cultural strangeness.

27
00:01:44.941 --> 00:01:47.710
Your turn. Say: foreignisation.

28
00:01:50.410 --> 00:01:51.550
foreignisation.

29
00:01:52.550 --> 00:01:53.783
localisation.

30
00:01:54.483 --> 00:01:59.142
localisation means adapting products or content for a particular market.

31
00:01:59.442 --> 00:02:03.194
Game localisation includes changing jokes and names.

32
00:02:03.894 --> 00:02:07.011
Your turn. Say: localisation.

33
00:02:09.711 --> 00:02:10.991
localisation.

34
00:02:11.983 --> 00:02:13.542
machine translation.

35
00:02:14.242 --> 00:02:19.829
machine translation means translation produced automatically by computers.

36
00:02:20.129 --> 00:02:23.011
Machine translation has improved dramatically.

37
00:02:23.711 --> 00:02:27.605
Your turn. Say: machine translation.

38
00:02:30.305 --> 00:02:31.910
machine translation.

39
00:02:32.917 --> 00:02:34.835
literary translation.

40
00:02:35.535 --> 00:02:41.134
literary translation means translating novels, poems and other literature.

41
00:02:41.434 --> 00:02:44.733
Literary translation is a creative art.

42
00:02:45.433 --> 00:02:48.922
Your turn. Say: literary translation.

43
00:02:51.622 --> 00:02:53.192
literary translation.

44
00:02:54.183 --> 00:02:56.427
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:02:56.727 --> 00:02:59.690
Rithy, can every word be translated?

46
00:03:00.983 --> 00:03:08.709
Not perfectly. The translation of Khmer kinship terms into English involves a considerable loss of nuance.

47
00:03:10.017 --> 00:03:12.086
So equivalence is never complete?

48
00:03:13.383 --> 00:03:20.006
Rarely. The translator's choice between fidelity and fluency determines the reader's experience.

49
00:03:21.317 --> 00:03:25.418
Venuti called that the tension between domestication and foreignisation.

50
00:03:26.717 --> 00:03:33.163
My preference is for foreignisation in literary translation; the preservation of strangeness matters.

51
00:03:34.450 --> 00:03:36.217
And machine translation?

52
00:03:37.517 --> 00:03:43.266
Its accuracy in everyday texts is impressive; its handling of humour and poetry, less so.

53
00:03:44.550 --> 00:03:47.931
Grammar focus: Lexical density (extended).

54
00:03:48.231 --> 00:03:52.216
Lexically dense writing uses nominal groups as subjects.

55
00:03:52.516 --> 00:03:58.753
The translation of kinship terms into English involves a loss of nuance.

56
00:03:59.053 --> 00:04:00.960
Its handling of humour is weaker.

57
00:04:01.260 --> 00:04:08.275
Dense nominal groups are efficient in academic prose; vary them with verbs to maintain readability.

58
00:04:09.283 --> 00:04:12.957
Now read and listen. Lost and Found in Translation.

59
00:04:13.257 --> 00:04:17.231
Translation is often described as the art of the impossible.

60
00:04:18.517 --> 00:04:22.536
No two languages divide the world in exactly the same way.

61
00:04:23.850 --> 00:04:29.890
Khmer, for example, encodes age and status in forms of address that English simply lacks.

62
00:04:31.183 --> 00:04:37.885
Translators must therefore balance fidelity to the original with fluency for new readers.

63
00:04:39.183 --> 00:04:47.731
Lawrence Venuti distinguished domestication, which makes texts feel familiar, from foreignisation, which preserves difference.

64
00:04:49.017 --> 00:04:53.756
Localisation extends these choices to games, software and advertising.

65
00:04:55.050 --> 00:05:03.992
Machine translation now handles routine texts remarkably well, though literary translation remains a profoundly human art.

66
00:05:05.283 --> 00:05:11.242
What is untranslatable, perhaps, is precisely what makes each language precious.

67
00:05:12.550 --> 00:05:15.237
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:05:15.537 --> 00:05:18.431
Faithfulness to the original text is blank.

69
00:05:18.731 --> 00:05:23.917
A, fidelity. B, localisation. C, equivalence.

70
00:05:27.917 --> 00:05:30.810
The answer is A: fidelity.

71
00:05:32.317 --> 00:05:34.572
Which is the most lexically dense?

72
00:05:34.872 --> 00:05:43.018
A, they translated it carefully. B, the careful translation of the text. C, it was translated.

73
00:05:47.017 --> 00:05:51.152
The answer is B: the careful translation of the text.

74
00:05:52.650 --> 00:05:55.790
'blank of jokes is the hardest part.'

75
00:05:56.090 --> 00:06:01.473
A, Translate. B, The translation. C, Translating of.

76
00:06:05.483 --> 00:06:08.493
The answer is B: The translation.

77
00:06:09.983 --> 00:06:12.749
Keeping the foreign feel of the original is blank.

78
00:06:13.049 --> 00:06:18.374
A, foreignisation. B, domestication. C, machine translation.

79
00:06:22.383 --> 00:06:24.801
The answer is A: foreignisation.

80
00:06:26.317 --> 00:06:30.371
According to the text, what does Khmer encode that English lacks?

81
00:06:30.671 --> 00:06:36.461
A, tense. B, age and status in forms of address. C, plural forms.

82
00:06:40.450 --> 00:06:44.400
The answer is B: age and status in forms of address.

83
00:06:45.917 --> 00:06:51.843
Now it's your turn. Part one: Will machine translation replace human translators?

84
00:06:57.143 --> 00:07:02.684
Part two: Use nominal style to compare two types of translation and conclude.

85
00:07:08.984 --> 00:07:23.667
The automation of routine translation is already well advanced. The translation of literature and humour, however, requires cultural judgement that machines lack. The replacement of human translators therefore seems unlikely in creative fields.

86
00:07:23.967 --> 00:07:26.326
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:27.317 --> 00:07:32.372
Great work today! You learned eight new words and lexical density (extended).

88
00:07:32.672 --> 00:07:37.171
Today's value is respect for culture. Be proud of our Khmer culture.

89
00:07:37.471 --> 00:07:40.750
See you in Unit twenty-four. Goodbye!

90
00:07:41.050 --> 00:07:43.586
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

